WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE
lesson_id: rl_mdp_core
generated: 2026-05-24

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
An agent. A goal. A frozen lake.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.181
The ice stretches between them — and the
ice does not cooperate.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.035
The agent cannot see ahead. It has no
teacher, no map, no instructions.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.152
The only thing it will ever receive is a
number —

00:00:19.202 --> 00:00:22.208
once, at the very end, if it reaches the
goal.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.284
That single number is its only guide.

00:00:25.334 --> 00:00:28.803
And the question is: can anything be
learned from that?

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.776
The agent sets out. Two steps — and it
falls through the ice.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.800
No message arrives. No hint. Nothing
points back toward a better path.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.301
Only a zero.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.184
That zero is not feedback in any rich
sense — it is the absence of feedback.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.734
The agent chose, the environment
responded,

00:00:54.784 --> 00:00:59.445
and the only signal is a number that says
nothing about what went wrong.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.395
There is no teacher watching, no
supervisor pointing to the mistake.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.777
The agent must learn anyway.

00:01:07.827 --> 00:01:11.547
That is the defining challenge of
reinforcement learning.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.840
The agent tries again — a different path,
more careful steps.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.748
But the ice has its own say.

00:01:21.798 --> 00:01:26.485
At each step, the agent chooses a
direction — and arrives somewhere else.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.960
The environment is stochastic.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:34.637
The agent's choice does not determine the
outcome; it only shifts the probabilities.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.837
A fall into the same hole. Another zero.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.288
Notice the gap between what the agent
intended and where it ended up.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.834
That gap — between intention and outcome —
is not a failure of the agent.

00:01:50.884 --> 00:01:52.891
It is a property of the world.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.693
The world does not bend to the agent's
will. The agent must account for that.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.429
One more attempt.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.640
Five steps across the ice — the agent
slips, recovers, slips again —

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.104
and finally reaches the far corner.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.197
And now something different happens.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.451
A reward of one.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.312
That is all. One number. No explanation of
which steps were good.

00:02:23.362 --> 00:02:25.912
No partial credit for the near-misses.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.187
Every zero along the way told the agent
nothing.

00:02:30.237 --> 00:02:33.677
Only the final step, at the goal, carries
a signal.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.132
This is called a sparse reward —

00:02:36.182 --> 00:02:40.272
and it is the norm in many real problems,
not the exception.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.315
A reward at the very end of a sequence
cannot be credited to any single action —

00:02:47.365 --> 00:02:52.731
the agent must figure out which actions
across all five steps actually mattered.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.264
How does an agent even begin to reason
about that?

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.602
The answer requires a precise language for
describing what the environment does and

00:03:03.652 --> 00:03:06.171
what the agent is trying to maximize.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.128
That language is the Markov Decision
Process.

00:03:11.178 --> 00:03:14.357
And it starts with a deceptively simple
idea.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.008
Every cell the agent can occupy is a
state.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.293
The frozen lake has sixteen of them —

00:03:23.343 --> 00:03:27.080
numbered zero at the top-left, fifteen at
the bottom-right.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.618
A state is a complete description of where
the agent is.

00:03:32.668 --> 00:03:35.485
Nothing else matters for what happens
next.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.515
Not the path that brought the agent here.
Not the number of steps taken.

00:03:40.565 --> 00:03:42.784
Not the history of failed attempts.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.188
Only the current state, combined with the
action the agent takes,

00:03:48.238 --> 00:03:50.976
determines what the environment does next.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.897
This is the Markov property —

00:03:53.947 --> 00:03:57.909
and it is both a simplification and an
enabling assumption.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.779
The simplification: we discard history,
and the problem becomes tractable.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.892
The enabling assumption: the state must
actually capture everything relevant.

00:04:10.942 --> 00:04:13.637
For FrozenLake, the cell number does that.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.691
Take state six — one of the safe cells in
the second row.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.232
Suppose the agent arrived here after two
steps. Or four steps.

00:04:25.282 --> 00:04:27.699
Or by a completely different route.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.323
It does not matter.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.265
The ice has no memory of how the agent got
here.

00:04:34.315 --> 00:04:40.136
The environment's response depends only on
where the agent is now and what it chooses

00:04:40.186 --> 00:04:40.877
to do now.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.453
The agent chooses RIGHT from state six.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.907
But the ice is slippery — "choosing right"
is not the same as "arriving right."

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.471
What actually happens next is governed by
a probability distribution over possible

00:04:57.521 --> 00:04:58.464
landing cells.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.206
The agent chose. The environment will
respond.

00:05:03.256 --> 00:05:05.755
But the response is not the action —

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:09.344
it is something the environment controls
entirely.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.136
So the natural question becomes:

00:05:13.186 --> 00:05:18.787
what is the precise mathematical object
that describes the environment's response?

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.693
This is the dynamics function — p of
s-prime, r, given s and a.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.460
It is a probability —

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:33.567
the chance that the agent lands in state
s-prime and receives reward r,

00:05:33.617 --> 00:05:37.069
given that it was in state s and chose
action a.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.800
Four arguments. Four roles. And they
divide the world cleanly in two.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.355
The agent controls a — the action. The
environment controls everything else.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.346
State s is where the agent is. The action
a is what it does.

00:05:55.396 --> 00:05:59.939
State s-prime is where it lands. Reward r
is what it receives.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.911
From state six, choosing RIGHT,

00:06:06.961 --> 00:06:10.440
the ice spreads the agent's landing across
three cells.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.139
Upward one row. Rightward one column.
Downward one row.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.883
Each destination carries the same
probability — one third.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:27.699
The agent cannot steer between them. The
choice of RIGHT has been made.

00:06:27.749 --> 00:06:29.421
The ice decides the rest.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.362
That one-third for each outcome is the
value of p for this state, this action,

00:06:35.412 --> 00:06:37.424
and each possible next state.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.962
The s-prime in the equation is not a fixed
destination —

00:06:43.012 --> 00:06:47.811
it is a variable that ranges over every
cell the agent might reach.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.532
One of those destinations is a hole.

00:06:51.582 --> 00:06:54.616
Landing there ends the episode
immediately.

00:06:54.666 --> 00:06:59.901
The reward is still zero — the hole
carries no penalty, only termination.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:06.853
The agent intended right. The ice gave it
three equally weighted possibilities.

00:07:06.903 --> 00:07:09.020
The equation p of s-prime, r,

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:13.129
given s and a is the formal name for that
uncertainty —

00:07:13.179 --> 00:07:16.616
every time, for every state, for every
action.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.568
This is what the agent is navigating.

00:07:20.618 --> 00:07:24.933
Not a fixed world — a distribution over
worlds at every step.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:33.483
The three outcomes from state six, action
RIGHT — made visible as a distribution.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:39.030
Each bar represents how likely the agent
ends up in that particular cell.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:44.368
One third for state two. One third for
state seven. One third for state ten.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.373
Equal heights. Equal probabilities. The
ice plays no favorites.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.732
The bars must sum to one —

00:07:52.782 --> 00:07:56.983
that is the normalization constraint on
the dynamics function.

00:07:57.033 --> 00:07:59.981
All probability mass must be accounted
for.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.555
State seven is a hole — reaching it ends
the episode.

00:08:05.605 --> 00:08:09.773
The probability of landing there is the
same as anywhere else.

00:08:09.823 --> 00:08:12.816
The world does not warn you before you
fall.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.642
Intention and outcome are two separate
events. The choice has been made.

00:08:18.692 --> 00:08:21.168
The distribution is what happens next.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.158
The same distribution — the same p of
s-prime, r, given s and a —

00:08:29.208 --> 00:08:34.496
lives inside the Gymnasium environment as
a Python data structure.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:40.145
env dot unwrapped dot P at six at two
addresses the environment's dynamics model

00:08:40.195 --> 00:08:43.832
directly: state six, action indexed two,
which is RIGHT.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.512
The outer loop ranges over every possible
outcome.

00:08:49.562 --> 00:08:52.005
Each iteration exposes four values:

00:08:52.055 --> 00:08:56.279
the probability of that outcome, the next
state, the reward,

00:08:56.329 --> 00:08:58.395
and whether the episode ends.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:04.215
Those four values are exactly the four
arguments of the dynamics function —

00:09:04.265 --> 00:09:08.899
just arranged as a Python tuple rather
than mathematical notation.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.692
For the first iteration:

00:09:11.742 --> 00:09:17.040
next state is ten, probability one third,
reward zero, episode continues.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:21.580
For the second: next state is seven — that
is the hole.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:25.787
Probability one third, reward zero, and
the episode terminates.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:27.939
For the third:

00:09:27.989 --> 00:09:33.144
next state is two, probability one third,
reward zero, episode continues.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.895
Three tuples. Three outcomes.

00:09:36.945 --> 00:09:41.037
The probabilities sum to one — the full
distribution is here.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:45.564
The code is not an approximation of the
mathematics.

00:09:45.614 --> 00:09:49.247
It is the same object, stored in a
computer's memory,

00:09:49.297 --> 00:09:51.451
accessible one key lookup away.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.291
The dynamics function is complete —

00:09:56.341 --> 00:10:00.955
summing over all next states and all
possible rewards must yield one.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:05.869
That is the normalization constraint:

00:10:05.919 --> 00:10:12.337
the sum over all s-prime and all r of p of
s-prime, r, given s and a, equals one —

00:10:12.387 --> 00:10:15.779
for every state and every available
action.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:20.652
Sometimes the reward structure is not what
we care about —

00:10:20.702 --> 00:10:24.851
we want to know only where the agent
lands, not what it receives.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.930
Summing the four-argument form over all
possible rewards gives the

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:35.280
state-transition probability: p of s-prime
given s and a.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:40.555
That derived form tells you the
probability of landing in s-prime.

00:10:40.605 --> 00:10:43.904
The four-argument form is the canonical
object —

00:10:43.954 --> 00:10:49.675
it contains strictly more information, and
every derived quantity follows from it.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:56.221
There is a second question we have not yet
answered.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:01.648
The dynamics function tells us where the
agent lands and what it receives —

00:11:01.698 --> 00:11:02.888
one step at a time.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:07.179
But what is the agent actually trying to
maximize?

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:13.928
Not the next reward. The return — the
weighted sum of all future rewards.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.604
G sub t equals R sub t-plus-one, plus
gamma times R sub t-plus-two,

00:11:20.654 --> 00:11:25.330
plus gamma-squared times R sub
t-plus-three, and so on —

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:27.018
which compresses to:

00:11:27.068 --> 00:11:32.588
the sum over k from zero to infinity of
gamma to the k times R sub

00:11:32.638 --> 00:11:34.157
t-plus-k-plus-one.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:39.229
Every reward in the agent's future
contributes to G sub t.

00:11:39.279 --> 00:11:43.655
Rewards further away are weighted by
higher powers of gamma.

00:11:43.705 --> 00:11:46.435
Nothing is ignored — only discounted.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.987
Walk through the successful path, step by
step.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:00.562
The agent starts at state zero. The first
step brings no reward.

00:12:00.612 --> 00:12:05.728
The second step brings no reward. The
third, fourth, fifth — all zeros.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:08.643
Five steps of nothing.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:13.510
The accumulator stays at zero through
every one of them —

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.995
because there is nothing to accumulate.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:21.971
Then, at the sixth step, the agent reaches
the goal. A reward of one arrives.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:27.568
But that reward did not arrive at step
one. It arrived at step six.

00:12:27.618 --> 00:12:30.168
And the return discounts by distance.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.643
With gamma equal to 0.99,

00:12:34.693 --> 00:12:41.967
a reward six steps away is worth 0.99 to
the fifth power times one —

00:12:42.017 --> 00:12:45.141
which is approximately 0.951.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:50.467
The reward is not lost. It reaches the
agent's objective in full —

00:12:50.517 --> 00:12:54.213
just reduced by the distance it traveled
through time.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:01.999
A patient agent with gamma close to one
treats a reward ten steps away almost as

00:13:02.049 --> 00:13:04.469
seriously as a reward right now.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:10.254
That patience is exactly what the agent
needs to value the distant goal on this

00:13:10.304 --> 00:13:14.131
frozen lake — where no reward arrives
until the very end.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.731
How much does the distance actually cost?

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.914
With gamma equal to 0.5 — the agent is
impatient.

00:13:24.964 --> 00:13:29.525
A reward five steps away is worth 0.5 to
the fifth power,

00:13:29.575 --> 00:13:33.053
which is roughly three cents on the
dollar.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:40.773
With gamma equal to 0.99, the same reward
is worth ninety-five cents.

00:13:40.823 --> 00:13:44.536
The agent is nearly as patient as it can
be.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:46.963
With gamma equal to 1.0 —

00:13:47.013 --> 00:13:52.168
every future reward counts at full value,
no discounting at all.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.373
FrozenLake always terminates.

00:13:56.423 --> 00:14:00.719
Every episode ends, either in a hole or at
the goal.

00:14:00.769 --> 00:14:03.810
So gamma equal to 1.0 is valid here —

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:09.125
the sum G sub t is always finite because
the episode is finite.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:14.524
But for tasks that run forever — tasks
with no natural endpoint —

00:14:14.574 --> 00:14:18.887
gamma strictly less than one is required.
Without discounting,

00:14:18.937 --> 00:14:23.440
the infinite sum of rewards may not
converge to a finite number.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.876
In this series, gamma is set to 0.99 by
convention —

00:14:28.926 --> 00:14:31.443
not because FrozenLake demands it,

00:14:31.493 --> 00:14:37.483
but to prepare for algorithms that must
handle continuing tasks where gamma less

00:14:37.533 --> 00:14:39.421
than one is not optional.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:44.373
The discount factor is the agent's
relationship with its own future.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:53.717
Look at the sum form of G sub t. Factor
out gamma from every term after the first.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:15:01.621
What remains is: R sub t-plus-one, plus
gamma times the quantity R sub t-plus-two,

00:15:01.671 --> 00:15:05.119
plus gamma times R sub t-plus-three, plus
—

00:15:05.169 --> 00:15:09.400
which is exactly the definition of G sub
t-plus-one.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.733
The entire infinite tail collapses into a
single term.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:20.717
G sub t equals R sub t-plus-one, plus
gamma times G sub t-plus-one.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:23.927
This is the return recursion.

00:15:23.977 --> 00:15:29.108
The return at any step equals the
immediate reward plus a discounted version

00:15:29.158 --> 00:15:31.749
of the return from the very next step.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.388
The recursion does not change what G sub t
means —

00:15:36.438 --> 00:15:39.551
it just expresses that meaning more
compactly,

00:15:39.601 --> 00:15:42.557
by folding the future into a single
symbol.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.045
We will see this identity again in the
next video —

00:15:47.095 --> 00:15:51.040
in a form that will let us reason about
what each state is worth.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:57.307
Two objects define a Markov Decision
Process.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:01.956
The dynamics function p of s-prime, r,
given s and a —

00:16:02.006 --> 00:16:04.256
the environment's response law,

00:16:04.306 --> 00:16:10.265
specifying for any state and action the
distribution over where you land and what

00:16:10.315 --> 00:16:11.205
you receive.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.993
The return G sub t — the agent's
objective,

00:16:16.043 --> 00:16:21.704
the discounted sum of everything that will
ever happen from this moment forward.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:28.547
The agent acts in a world governed by p.
It tries to maximize G sub t.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:31.152
Everything else in this series —

00:16:31.202 --> 00:16:34.660
every algorithm, every theorem, every
update rule —

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:37.256
is built on top of these two objects.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:42.697
Next: we give the agent a strategy for
choosing actions —

00:16:42.747 --> 00:16:46.232
and ask what each state is worth under
that strategy.
